[' The Newman-Shanks-Williams (NSW) prime number algorithm is a deterministic algorithm that can be used to find prime numbers of the form $2^p + 1$, where $p$ is also a prime number. The $n$th NSW prime number can be found using the following function:
```
function nsw_prime(n) {
  if (n == 1) {
    return 3; // the first NSW prime is 3
  }

  // Compute the (n-1)th NSW prime
  var p = nsw_prime(n-1);

  // Compute the nth NSW prime
  var q = p + (p >> 1) + 1;

  // Check if q is prime
  if (is_prime(q)) {
    return q;
  }

  // If q is not prime, try again with a different value of p
  return nsw_prime(n);
}

function is_prime(n) {
  // Check if n is less than or equal to 1
  if (n <= 1) {
    return false;
  }

  // Check if n is divisible by 2 or 3
  if (n % 2 == 0 || n % 3 == 0) {
    return false;
  }

  // Check if n is a power of 2
  if (n == 2^k for some integer k) {
    return false;
  }

  // n is prime
  return true;
}
```
This function uses a recursive approach to find the $n$th NSW prime number. It starts by checking if $n=1$, in which case the first NSW prime is 3. Otherwise, it computes the $(n-1)$th NSW prime using the same function, and then computes the $n$th NSW prime as $p + (p>>1) + 1$, where $p$ is the $(n-1)$th NSW prime. It then checks if the $n$th NSW prime is prime using the `is_prime` function, and if it is not, it recursively calls itself to try again with a different value of $p$.

The `is_prime` function is a simple implementation that checks if a number is prime by checking if it is less than or equal to 1, divisible by 2 or 3, or a power of 2. If it is not any of these, it must be prime.

Note that this implementation assumes that the input $n$ is a positive integer. If negative integers are allowed, additional checks would be needed to handle the case where $n$ is negative.']

